Make the margins explicit so the plot area is known, then solve the px↔unit circularity once:

```js
// mosaic: real bands live on the season scale; an empty band gets a FIXED-PX
// gutter (never a season-unit) so it can never read as a 1-season band and its
// label stays legible however long the window is.
const MT = 6, MB = 30;                       // must match the Plot.plot spec below
const bands   = rowDomain.filter((g) => g !== "All years");
const emptyN  = bands.filter((g) => !totalOf.has(g)).length;
const bandTotal = d3.sum(bands, (g) => totalOf.get(g) ?? 0);
const refH = Math.max(2, bandTotal * 0.1);
const gap  = Math.max(1, refH * 0.35);
const plotPx = H - MT - MB;
// 3 wrapped lines at fontSize 10.5 / lineHeight 1.1 ≈ 35 px; cap the total
// gutter so a mostly-empty panel still leaves room for the real bands
const gutter = emptyN ? Math.min(36, (plotPx * 0.4) / emptyN) : 0;
const scaleUnits = bandTotal + (rowDomain.includes("All years") ? gap + refH : 0);
const unitPx = Math.max(1e-6, (plotPx - emptyN * gutter) / scaleUnits);
const hEmpty = gutter / unitPx;              // fixed px expressed in scale units
const pos = new Map();
let yEnd = 0;
for (const g of bands) {
  const h = totalOf.has(g) ? totalOf.get(g) : hEmpty;
  pos.set(g, [yEnd, yEnd + h]);
  yEnd += h;
}
```

and add `marginTop: MT, marginBottom: MB` to the Mosaic `Plot.plot({...})` call (line 906) so `plotPx` is exact rather than relying on Plot's auto margins (measured 0.5/30 today).

